module gunbc.fleet_known_hosts_anchor


// THE TRUST ANCHOR AN SSH INVOCATION IS VERIFIED AGAINST, carried as a value rather than left as
// ambient host state.
//
// WHAT THIS REPLACES, and why the replacement is a different construction rather than a repair of
// the old one. fleet_host_key_enroll_apply materialized a staged fragment, then appended a `Host`
// block to $HOME/.ssh/config naming it, and read back with `ssh-keygen -F` against THE STAGED
// FRAGMENT. Three independent defects, all of them the same shape -- a check standing at a
// different place from the binding it claims to establish:
//
//   ONE, the readback interrogated the fragment the writer had just written, not the file ssh would
//   actually consult. The two are related only by a path string appearing in two places, and on the
//   live fleet they DID diverge: the config named a run-specific absolute path baked from $(pwd) of
//   whichever runner slot wrote it first, while nine other slots sharing that same $HOME resolved it
//   to a file that did not exist for them. Every readback reported found; every probe ran unverified.
//
//   TWO, $HOME/.ssh is SHARED MUTABLE STATE on a self-hosted runner -- ten slots, one ghrunner
//   account -- so the artifact one attempt writes is read by an unrelated attempt. A marker guard
//   (`grep -q gunbc-fleet-pin`) made it worse rather than safer: it froze the FIRST writer's content
//   permanently while every later run reported success without changing anything.
//
//   THREE, the outcome was PROSE. fleet_host_key_enroll_apply returned a String and its caller
//   decided by string_contains(receipt, "outcome=refused"), so a refusal was a substring -- a
//   spelling change silently turns a refusal into a success, and nothing structurally prevents an
//   SSH leg from running after one.
//
// THE ANCHOR IS CONTENT-BOUND. Both identities are computed, one from the registry rows and one
// from bytes READ BACK off the filesystem, and the record cannot be constructed unless they agree.
// So "the file ssh will consult contains exactly the enrolled registry" is not a property some
// witness asserts about this module -- it is the precondition of the value existing at all. There
// is no path from an empty write, a partial write, a stale file, or another attempt's file to a
// KnownHostsAnchor.
//
// THE PATH IS ATTEMPT-OWNED AND CONTENT-ADDRESSED, in that order, and the earlier revision of this
// paragraph got it wrong in a way worth recording. It argued that a purely content-addressed path
// needed no attempt scoping, because two runs holding the same registry write identical bytes and so
// have nothing to race over. That argument is false: identical content does not establish
// IMMUTABILITY. A later writer can modify the path after another attempt has already sealed its
// anchor, a read can observe a partial write unless the realization proves atomic replacement, and
// the sealed value therefore proves what was read AT CONSTRUCTION -- not what ssh will read when the
// leg actually runs. Nothing in a shared mutable path carries an ownership boundary.
//
// So the attempt owns the FILENAME and the digest names the content within it. A flat
// attempt-prefixed name rather than an attempt-owned directory, for a reason worth stating rather
// than leaving as an unexplained shape: extdeps.filesystem.filesystem_io models Write, Read, Delete
// and List, and no directory-creation operation, so a nested path would refuse on every first use.
// The isolation property is identical either way -- it is "no other attempt writes this path", which
// a unique prefix satisfies exactly as a directory does. What the directory would additionally have
// bought is cleanup as one removal, which this module does not yet perform in either shape; adding a
// filesystem capability to obtain a nicer shape for cleanup that does not exist would be building
// the second thing to justify the first. The digest keeps the filename a claim about the content
// that the readback checks. Both parts live under the repository writable working root, never under
// $HOME, so the shared-runner defect has no surface left either.
//
// The lifetime is still not bracketed to the SSH operation: nothing deletes the directory when the
// legs finish, so a stale attempt directory can outlive its attempt. That is a real remaining gap,
// not a closed one -- it is bounded by the attempt segment (a stale directory can never be READ by a
// different attempt, only left behind) but it is cleanup this module does not yet perform.
type KnownHostsAnchor sole_constructor {
  path: std.types.NonEmptyStr
  registry_identity: std.content_hash.ContentHash
  materialized_content_identity: std.content_hash.ContentHash
  attempt: FleetSshAttemptIdentity
}

// THE ATTEMPT THAT OWNS THE MATERIALIZATION. A required parameter rather than something this module
// invents, because an identity a function mints for itself cannot distinguish two concurrent callers
// -- and the caller is the only party that knows what an attempt IS here (a CI job, a dispatched
// convergence, one operator invocation). Making it explicit also keeps it out of ambient state,
// which is the failure the whole anchor exists to close.
//
// IT IS A PATH SEGMENT, and the earlier revision wrapping a bare NonEmptyStr in a record was the
// nicknaming defect: a record field named `value` carrying a String says nothing the String did not
// already say, so it minted a second name for a concept without grounding it. What the value
// actually IS is decided by its use -- it is concatenated into target/fleet-ssh-attempt-<id>/ -- so
// as a bare string it was neither path-safe nor wire-safe, and an attempt id containing a slash or a
// `..` would have escaped the directory that is supposed to be its ownership boundary. This is the
// same grounding gunbc.merge_admission_subject WalkAttemptId already reached for the same reason,
// and reaching for it a second way rather than reusing the shape would itself be the fork.
//
// fleet_ssh_attempt_identity is the only constructor and REFUSES rather than sanitizing: a caller
// whose attempt id is unsafe has a naming problem this module cannot resolve on their behalf, and
// quietly rewriting it would hand back an identity that is not the one they hold.
type FleetSshAttemptIdentity = std.types.PathSegment where brand("FleetSshAttemptIdentity")

fn fleet_ssh_attempt_identity(raw: String) -> FleetSshAttemptIdentity? {
  if std.types.path_segment_is_safe(raw: raw) {
    Present { value: raw as FleetSshAttemptIdentity }
  } else {
    none
  }
}

// The sole mint, admitted to exactly one production declaration. Sealing matters here for the same
// reason it matters on TransportScript: without it, any caller could author a KnownHostsAnchor
// naming any path with any two identities it chose, and the content binding above would be a
// convention rather than a wall. A record literal in another module is exactly the forgery the
// content check exists to prevent, one layer up.
fn known_hosts_anchor_seal(
  path: std.types.NonEmptyStr,
  registry_identity: std.content_hash.ContentHash,
  materialized_content_identity: std.content_hash.ContentHash,
  attempt: FleetSshAttemptIdentity,
) -> KnownHostsAnchor
  admit_callers: [
    std.decl_ref.decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fleet_known_hosts_anchor", decl_name: "materialize_fleet_known_hosts_anchor"),
    std.decl_ref.decl_ref(module_path: "test.claim.typed_remote_file_write_fleet_ssh_fixture", decl_name: "known_hosts_anchor_hermetic_witness_fixture"),
  ]
  = KnownHostsAnchor {
    path: path,
    registry_identity: registry_identity,
    materialized_content_identity: materialized_content_identity,
    attempt: attempt,
  }

// The typed outcome that replaces the prose receipt. AlreadyConverged is a genuinely distinct state
// from Ready and is kept separate rather than collapsed: it is what a repeated run on a warm working
// root reports, and fusing it into Ready would make "we wrote the file" and "the file was already
// exactly right" indistinguishable -- which is precisely the distinction the marker-guard defect
// destroyed. Both carry an anchor, because in both cases the content binding was established by a
// readback; only the write differs.
//
// KnownHostsRefused is head-plus-tail, so an empty refusal has no constructor: a refusal that names
// no cause cannot be built, which is the shape the corpus uses wherever a diagnostic must be
// non-empty by construction.
type KnownHostsMaterializationOutcome
  = KnownHostsReady { anchor: KnownHostsAnchor, receipt: String }
  | KnownHostsAlreadyConverged { anchor: KnownHostsAnchor, receipt: String }
  | KnownHostsRefused { first: String, more: List<String> }

// CANONICAL, not registry-order. An earlier revision hashed the rows in whatever order the list
// happened to carry, which made the identity -- and therefore the anchor's filename -- a function of
// the LIST rather than of the registry it denotes. Two authors reordering the same six enrollments
// would have produced two different anchors for the same set of trusted keys, and the digest would
// have reported them as different registries. That is the list-identity defect the corpus has hit
// before: a mathematical set given an identity that depends on its traversal.
//
// Sorting the rendered lines is the canonicalization rather than sorting the rows, because the LINE
// is what ssh consumes -- so canonical bytes and canonical identity are the same operation, and the
// emitted file is order-independent too rather than only its hash.
fn canonical_known_hosts_lines(rows: List<gunbc.fleet_host_key_enrollment.KnownHostKeyRow>) -> List<String> {
  map(rows, k => gunbc.fleet_host_key_enrollment.known_hosts_line(k: k)) |> sort_by(line => line)
}

fn canonical_known_hosts_fragment(rows: List<gunbc.fleet_host_key_enrollment.KnownHostKeyRow>) -> String {
  join(map(canonical_known_hosts_lines(rows: rows), l => concat(l, "\n")), "")
}

fn canonical_known_hosts_identity(rows: List<gunbc.fleet_host_key_enrollment.KnownHostKeyRow>) -> std.content_hash.ContentHash {
  std.content_hash.content_hash_of_value(value: canonical_known_hosts_fragment(rows: rows) as std.types.NonEmptyStr)
}

// A DUPLICATE ENDPOINT IS A CONFLICT, not a redundancy. known_hosts accepts a host key matching ANY
// line for that endpoint, so two rows naming one endpoint with different keys widen what is trusted
// while the registry reads as if it pinned one key -- and the digest would happily identify that
// widened set. Counted at endpoint grain because the endpoint is what ssh matches on.
fn known_hosts_endpoint_conflict(rows: List<gunbc.fleet_host_key_enrollment.KnownHostKeyRow>) -> String? {
  let endpoints = map(rows, k => k.endpoint) |> sort_by(e => e)
  fold(endpoints, init: none, f: fn(acc, e) {
    match acc {
      Present { value: _ } => acc
      Absent => if length(filter(endpoints, x => x == e)) > 1 {
        Present { value: join(["endpoint ", e, " is enrolled more than once, so known_hosts would accept any of its keys while the registry reads as pinning one"], "") }
      } else { none }
    }
  })
}

fn fleet_known_hosts_fragment() -> String {
  canonical_known_hosts_fragment(rows: gunbc.fleet_host_key_enrollment.fleet_host_key_enrollments)
}

fn fleet_known_hosts_registry_identity() -> std.content_hash.ContentHash {
  canonical_known_hosts_identity(rows: gunbc.fleet_host_key_enrollment.fleet_host_key_enrollments)
}

// The filename carries the registry digest, so the path is a claim the readback then verifies.
fn fleet_known_hosts_anchor_path(attempt: FleetSshAttemptIdentity) -> std.types.NonEmptyStr {
  join([
    "target/fleet-ssh-attempt-", attempt as String,
    "-known-hosts-", std.content_hash.serialize_content_hash(hash: fleet_known_hosts_registry_identity()) as String,
  ], "") as std.types.NonEmptyStr
}

// THREE THINGS THE BODY DOES THAT ARE NOT OBVIOUS FROM ITS SHAPE, stated here because DESIGN 4c
// admits annotations only at module-item grain. The existing file is READ BEFORE the write, so a
// file already carrying exactly these bytes is reported as already-converged rather than rewritten
// -- a repeated run stays legible instead of silently idempotent, which is the distinction the
// marker guard destroyed. The readback is then a SECOND read even on that already-converged path,
// so the identity landing in the anchor is always computed from bytes observed after the decision to
// keep them, never from the value the writer held in hand. And the cross-family arm is unreachable
// while both sides are minted by content_hash_of_value, yet is stated as a refusal rather than
// folded into the equal arm regardless: a cross-family answer means the comparison did not happen,
// and "we could not compare" must never render as "they matched".

// THE MATERIALIZATION. Refuses before any write when the registry is empty (enrolling nothing must
// never read as enrolled), writes the fragment, then READS THE FILE BACK and mints only on digest
// agreement. The readback is the success criterion; the write's exit code is not, because a
// successful write to the wrong place is exactly the failure mode this replaces.
func materialize_fleet_known_hosts_anchor(attempt: FleetSshAttemptIdentity) -> KnownHostsMaterializationOutcome
  uses net: std.resources.Network
{
  if length(gunbc.fleet_host_key_enrollment.fleet_host_key_enrollments) == 0 {
    KnownHostsRefused {
      first: "known-hosts materialization refused: the enrollment registry is empty, so there is no host key to anchor to and every fleet SSH leg would be unverified",
      more: [],
    }
  } else { match known_hosts_endpoint_conflict(rows: gunbc.fleet_host_key_enrollment.fleet_host_key_enrollments) {
    Present { value: conflict } => KnownHostsRefused {
      first: join(["known-hosts materialization refused: ", conflict], ""),
      more: [],
    }
    Absent => {
    let fragment = fleet_known_hosts_fragment()
    let path = fleet_known_hosts_anchor_path(attempt: attempt)
    let registry_identity = fleet_known_hosts_registry_identity()
    let existing = Filesystem.Read(path: path as String)
    let already = existing.success && existing.content == fragment
    let written = if already { true } else {
      let w = Filesystem.Write(path: path as String, content: fragment)
      w.success
    }
    if written {
      let back = Filesystem.Read(path: path as String)
      if back.success {
        let materialized_identity = std.content_hash.content_hash_of_value(value: back.content as std.types.NonEmptyStr)
        match std.content_hash.compare_content_hash(left: registry_identity, right: materialized_identity) {
          std.content_hash.ContentHashEqual => {
            let anchor = known_hosts_anchor_seal(
              path: path,
              registry_identity: registry_identity,
              materialized_content_identity: materialized_identity,
              attempt: attempt,
            )
            let receipt = join(
              concat(
                [join([
                  "known-hosts-anchor path=", path as String,
                  " attempt=", attempt as String, " identity=", std.content_hash.serialize_content_hash(hash: registry_identity) as String,
                  " rows=", to_string(length(gunbc.fleet_host_key_enrollment.fleet_host_key_enrollments)), "\n",
                ], "")],
                map(gunbc.fleet_host_key_enrollment.fleet_host_key_enrollments, k => join([
                  "  enrolled host=", k.host as String, " endpoint=", k.endpoint,
                  " provenance=", gunbc.fleet_host_key_enrollment.host_key_provenance_label(p: k.provenance), "\n",
                ], "")),
              ),
              "",
            )
            if already {
              KnownHostsAlreadyConverged { anchor: anchor, receipt: receipt }
            } else {
              KnownHostsReady { anchor: anchor, receipt: receipt }
            }
          }
          std.content_hash.ContentHashDifferent => KnownHostsRefused {
            first: join([
              "known-hosts materialization refused: the bytes read back from ", path as String,
              " do not hash-equal the enrollment registry (registry ",
              std.content_hash.serialize_content_hash(hash: registry_identity) as String,
              ", materialized ", std.content_hash.serialize_content_hash(hash: materialized_identity) as String,
              ") -- the file ssh would consult is not the registry, which is the exact divergence the",
              " staged-fragment readback could not see",
            ], ""),
            more: [],
          }
          std.content_hash.ContentHashCrossFamilyIncomparable => KnownHostsRefused {
            first: "known-hosts materialization refused: registry and materialized identities are in different hash families and are therefore incomparable, so agreement was not established",
            more: [],
          }
        }
      } else {
        KnownHostsRefused {
          first: join(["known-hosts materialization refused: readback of ", path as String, " failed -- ", std.algebra.trim(s: back.error)], ""),
          more: [],
        }
      }
    } else {
      KnownHostsRefused {
        first: join(["known-hosts materialization refused: could not write the anchor at ", path as String], ""),
        more: [],
      }
    }
  } } }
}

// The one projection consumers need: the anchor path, reachable ONLY from an outcome that carries an
// anchor. There is no accessor that produces a path from a refusal, so a caller cannot obtain
// something to hand ssh without having passed the content binding.
fn known_hosts_anchor_of(outcome: KnownHostsMaterializationOutcome) -> KnownHostsAnchor? {
  match outcome {
    KnownHostsReady { anchor: a, receipt: _ } => Present { value: a }
    KnownHostsAlreadyConverged { anchor: a, receipt: _ } => Present { value: a }
    KnownHostsRefused { first: _, more: _ } => none
  }
}

fn known_hosts_materialization_receipt(outcome: KnownHostsMaterializationOutcome) -> String {
  match outcome {
    KnownHostsReady { anchor: _, receipt: r } => r
    KnownHostsAlreadyConverged { anchor: _, receipt: r } => concat("already-converged ", r)
    KnownHostsRefused { first: f, more: m } =>
      join(concat([concat("refused ", f)], map(m, x => concat("\nrefused ", x))), "")
  }
}

// THE WALL. openssh_exclusive_known_hosts_trust_options is a builder returning a List, and a List is
// not a wall: a caller may prepend its own option, and OpenSSH takes the FIRST obtained value for
// each keyword, so an earlier StrictHostKeyChecking=no silently outranks everything the builder
// produced. The failure would be invisible at the call site -- the correct options are all still
// there, just outranked.
//
// FleetOpenSshTrustPolicy closes that by being the thing consumers accept. It is sealed, so no
// module can author one; its sole mint takes a KnownHostsAnchor, which is itself sealed and
// obtainable only from a materialization whose readback hash-equalled the registry; and the options
// are DERIVED from it rather than carried, so there is no field for a caller to widen and no
// position for a caller to prepend into. The chain is unbroken: options exist only because a policy
// exists, a policy exists only because an anchor exists, and an anchor exists only because bytes on
// disk were observed to be the enrolled registry.
type FleetOpenSshTrustPolicy sole_constructor {
  anchor: KnownHostsAnchor
}

fn fleet_openssh_trust_policy_seal(anchor: KnownHostsAnchor) -> FleetOpenSshTrustPolicy
  admit_callers: [
    std.decl_ref.decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fleet_known_hosts_anchor", decl_name: "fleet_openssh_trust_policy_of"),
    std.decl_ref.decl_ref(module_path: "test.claim.typed_remote_file_write_fleet_ssh_fixture", decl_name: "fleet_openssh_trust_policy_hermetic_witness_fixture"),
  ]
  = FleetOpenSshTrustPolicy { anchor: anchor }

// The only route from an outcome to a policy. A refusal yields Absent, so a caller holding a refused
// materialization has nothing to hand an SSH leg -- which is what makes "on refusal, zero SSH legs
// execute" a property of the type rather than a discipline the caller is trusted to observe.
fn fleet_openssh_trust_policy_of(outcome: KnownHostsMaterializationOutcome) -> FleetOpenSshTrustPolicy? {
  match known_hosts_anchor_of(outcome: outcome) {
    Present { value: a } => Present { value: fleet_openssh_trust_policy_seal(anchor: a) }
    Absent => none
  }
}

// Derived, never stored. The anchor path is read off the sealed policy, so the options a leg carries
// and the file whose content was verified are the same fact rather than two facts a path string
// relates.
fn fleet_openssh_trust_options(policy: FleetOpenSshTrustPolicy) -> List<extdeps.ssh.client_options.SshClientOption> {
  extdeps.ssh.client_options.openssh_exclusive_known_hosts_trust_options(known_hosts: policy.anchor.path)
}

// ── The execution context ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// THE CREDENTIAL THE CLIENT AUTHENTICATES WITH, bound rather than ambient. The host-key half of this
// module establishes which machine we reached; this establishes which key reached it. For the Spark
// question -- which POSIX account accepts the fleet key -- an unbound credential makes every positive
// result ambiguous: an agent holding several identities can produce a success no modeled fact caused.
// TWO WAYS TO HOLD ONE CREDENTIAL, and they are separate arms rather than one record with an
// optional field because the OpenSSH posture that makes each EXACT is different in kind: the
// identity-file arm removes the agent entirely (`IdentityAgent=none`), while the agent arm names a
// socket and uses a PUBLIC key file purely as a selector. Fusing them would force one arm to carry a
// field the other must be trusted to ignore.
//
// This coproduct is why the Spark CI route could not previously carry trust facts at all: the binding
// required an identity file, an agent-held key has none, so `SparkServingSliceRunnerAgent` fell back
// to a bare `SshShell` with no anchor, no credential and no posture.
// WHERE the private half lives. This is a plain coproduct and the SEAL stays on the binding record
// below rather than moving here, deliberately: `sole_constructor` seals a record, so turning the
// binding itself into a coproduct would have made every arm publicly constructible and dropped the
// wall this module exists to hold. A forged locus is inert -- it names a socket or a file and
// nothing else -- while a forged BINDING would be a credential claim nobody observed.
type FleetSshCredentialLocus =
    FleetSshIdentityFileHeld { identity_file: std.types.NonEmptyStr }
  | FleetSshAgentHeld { agent_socket: std.types.NonEmptyStr, public_selector_file: std.types.NonEmptyStr }

type FleetSshCredentialBinding sole_constructor {
  locus: FleetSshCredentialLocus
  credential_identity: std.content_hash.ContentHash
}

// The identity of the public key material that was OBSERVED, whichever locus carried it. Receipts
// read this rather than matching, so adding a locus cannot silently produce a receipt with no
// identity in it.
fn fleet_ssh_credential_identity(binding: FleetSshCredentialBinding) -> std.content_hash.ContentHash {
  binding.credential_identity
}

// WHERE the credential was held, for a receipt. A reader must be able to tell an identity-file run
// from an agent run without inferring it from which function was called, because the two have
// different preconditions and different failure modes.
fn fleet_ssh_credential_locus_label(binding: FleetSshCredentialBinding) -> String {
  match binding.locus {
    FleetSshIdentityFileHeld { identity_file: f } =>
      join(["identity_file=", f as String, " agent=suppressed"], "")
    FleetSshAgentHeld { agent_socket: s, public_selector_file: p } =>
      join(["agent_socket=", s as String, " public_selector_file=", p as String], "")
  }
}

// Derived, never stored beside the locus: the options a leg carries come from the locus that was
// minted, so a caller cannot pair an agent-held credential with identity-file options or the reverse.
fn fleet_ssh_credential_options(binding: FleetSshCredentialBinding) -> List<extdeps.ssh.client_options.SshClientOption> {
  match binding.locus {
    FleetSshIdentityFileHeld { identity_file: f } =>
      extdeps.ssh.client_options.openssh_exact_credential_options(identity_file: f)
    FleetSshAgentHeld { agent_socket: s, public_selector_file: p } =>
      extdeps.ssh.client_options.openssh_exact_agent_credential_options(agent_socket: s, public_selector_file: p)
  }
}

// The mint is sealed for the same reason the anchor's is. The credential_identity is the identity of
// the PUBLIC key material, never of the private key: a receipt naming which credential authenticated
// must be publishable, and a digest of secret bytes in a receipt is credential material wearing a
// hash.
fn fleet_ssh_credential_binding_seal(
  locus: FleetSshCredentialLocus,
  credential_identity: std.content_hash.ContentHash,
) -> FleetSshCredentialBinding
  admit_callers: [
    std.decl_ref.decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fleet_known_hosts_anchor", decl_name: "fleet_ssh_credential_binding_of_derived_material"),
    std.decl_ref.decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fleet_known_hosts_anchor", decl_name: "fleet_ssh_agent_credential_binding_of_listed_key"),
  ]
  = FleetSshCredentialBinding { locus: locus, credential_identity: credential_identity }

// THE ONLY MINT, and it takes material DERIVED FROM THE IDENTITY FILE rather than material a caller
// declares alongside it. The superseded route (`fleet_ssh_credential_binding_of_public_material`)
// took `identity_file` and `public_material` as two independent parameters and hashed the second,
// so the binding asserted a correspondence nothing had observed: rotate the secret behind the file
// and every receipt naming which credential authenticated stays byte-identical and false. That is
// the §5 tell exactly -- the check was satisfiable by editing the declaration while the realization
// lied -- and the corpus already knew it, because `gunbc.ci_spec` `gunbc_ci_fleet_key_agent_script`
// performs this derivation in hand-authored shell on the OTHER route. Two answers to one question,
// one of them unfounded; this is the single authority, and that shell fragment's check becomes the
// second representation (see `fleet_ssh_credential_shell_verification_supersession_note`).
//
// Admitted to the classifier alone, which reaches this only on an observed match, so a binding
// cannot exist without a derivation that produced the material being hashed.
fn fleet_ssh_credential_binding_of_derived_material(
  identity_file: std.types.NonEmptyStr,
  derived_material: std.types.NonEmptyStr,
) -> FleetSshCredentialBinding
  admit_callers: [
    std.decl_ref.decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fleet_ssh_credential_verify", decl_name: "classify_derived_public_key_line"),
  ]
  = fleet_ssh_credential_binding_seal(
    locus: FleetSshIdentityFileHeld { identity_file: identity_file },
    credential_identity: std.content_hash.content_hash_of_value(value: derived_material),
  )

// The agent arm's mint, admitted to the agent classifier alone. The identity hashed is the material
// of the key the AGENT was observed to hold -- the classifier reaches this only after finding the
// declared fingerprint in the agent's own listing -- so it is the same kind of fact as the
// identity-file arm's, obtained from a different observation.
//
// The selector file carries the PUBLIC key and is what makes `IdentitiesOnly` select one identity out
// of an agent that may hold several. It is not a secret and is not what authenticates.
fn fleet_ssh_agent_credential_binding_of_listed_key(
  agent_socket: std.types.NonEmptyStr,
  public_selector_file: std.types.NonEmptyStr,
  listed_material: std.types.NonEmptyStr,
) -> FleetSshCredentialBinding
  admit_callers: [
    std.decl_ref.decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fleet_ssh_credential_verify", decl_name: "classify_agent_key_listing"),
  ]
  = fleet_ssh_credential_binding_seal(
    locus: FleetSshAgentHeld {
      agent_socket: agent_socket,
      public_selector_file: public_selector_file,
    },
    credential_identity: std.content_hash.content_hash_of_value(value: listed_material),
  )

// ONE VALUE CARRYING EVERYTHING AN SSH LEG NEEDS TO BE ACCOUNTABLE. Sealed, so it cannot be
// assembled beside the walls rather than through them, and constructible only from an anchor that
// passed its content readback plus a bound credential.
type FleetSshExecutionContext sole_constructor {
  attempt: FleetSshAttemptIdentity
  trust: FleetOpenSshTrustPolicy
  credential: FleetSshCredentialBinding
}

fn fleet_ssh_execution_context_seal(
  attempt: FleetSshAttemptIdentity,
  trust: FleetOpenSshTrustPolicy,
  credential: FleetSshCredentialBinding,
) -> FleetSshExecutionContext
  admit_callers: [
    std.decl_ref.decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fleet_known_hosts_anchor", decl_name: "fleet_ssh_execution_context_of"),
    std.decl_ref.decl_ref(module_path: "test.claim.typed_remote_file_write_fleet_ssh_fixture", decl_name: "fleet_ssh_execution_context_hermetic_witness_fixture"),
  ]
  = FleetSshExecutionContext { attempt: attempt, trust: trust, credential: credential }

// The only route from a materialization to a context. Absent on refusal, and there is no other
// constructor, so a caller whose trust preparation failed holds nothing it could hand an SSH leg.
fn fleet_ssh_execution_context_of(
  outcome: KnownHostsMaterializationOutcome,
  credential: FleetSshCredentialBinding,
) -> FleetSshExecutionContext? {
  match known_hosts_anchor_of(outcome: outcome) {
    Present { value: a } => Present {
      value: fleet_ssh_execution_context_seal(
        attempt: a.attempt,
        trust: fleet_openssh_trust_policy_seal(anchor: a),
        credential: credential,
      ),
    }
    Absent => none
  }
}

// WHO WE ARE TALKING TO, as a principal at an endpoint rather than a pre-joined string. The probe's
// whole product is a claim about a (host, principal) pair, so fusing them into one "user@host" text
// at the call site would put the thing being measured inside an unparseable leaf.
type SshTarget {
  endpoint: std.types.NonEmptyStr
  principal: std.types.NonEmptyStr
}

// A REMOTE WORD THAT SURVIVES THE REMOTE SHELL. The transported command is one string which the
// remote login shell re-parses, so a word carrying a space, quote, redirect, expansion or separator
// is not the word that executes. Sealed and admitted through one predicate, so "portable" is a
// property of the value rather than a promise in a function name -- which is what the previous
// List<String> parameter was.
type PortableRemoteWord sole_constructor {
  value: std.types.NonEmptyStr
}

fn portable_remote_word_char_allowed(cp: Int) -> Bool {
  (cp >= 97 && cp <= 122) || (cp >= 65 && cp <= 90) || (cp >= 48 && cp <= 57)
    || cp == 46 || cp == 95 || cp == 45 || cp == 47 || cp == 64 || cp == 58 || cp == 61
}

fn portable_remote_word_text_ok(text: String, i: Int, len: Int) -> Bool {
  if i >= len { true } else {
    if portable_remote_word_char_allowed(cp: code_point(ch: char_at(text, i))) {
      portable_remote_word_text_ok(text: text, i: i + 1, len: len)
    } else { false }
  }
}

// Refuses rather than quoting: quoting would make the word survive THIS shell, and the remote shell
// is not known to be the same one. The allowlist is the same conservative alphabet the existing
// typed-argv edge already enforces, reused rather than re-spelled.
fn portable_remote_word(raw: String) -> PortableRemoteWord? {
  if raw != "" && portable_remote_word_text_ok(text: raw, i: 0, len: length(raw)) {
    Present { value: PortableRemoteWord { value: raw as std.types.NonEmptyStr } }
  } else {
    none
  }
}

// Head-plus-tail, so an empty remote command has no constructor: an SSH leg with no words is an
// interactive login, which is a different operation from running a command and must not be reachable
// by passing an empty list to this one.
type PortableRemoteWords {
  first: PortableRemoteWord
  more: List<PortableRemoteWord>
}

fn portable_remote_words(raws: List<String>) -> PortableRemoteWords? {
  let admitted = fold(raws, init: Present { value: [] }, f: fn(acc, r) {
    match acc {
      Absent => none
      Present { value: ws } => match portable_remote_word(raw: r) {
        Present { value: w } => Present { value: concat(ws, [w]) }
        Absent => none
      }
    }
  })
  match admitted {
    Absent => none
    Present { value: ws } => match v2.std.algebra.list_head(ws) {
      v2.std.algebra.HeadAbsent => none
      HeadFound { value: h } => match v2.std.algebra.list_tail(ws) {
        TailFound { tail: t } => Present { value: PortableRemoteWords { first: h, more: t } }
        v2.std.algebra.TailAbsent => Present { value: PortableRemoteWords { first: h, more: [] } }
      }
    }
  }
}

fn portable_remote_words_strings(words: PortableRemoteWords) -> List<String> {
  concat([words.first.value as String], map(words.more, w => w.value as String))
}

// THE TERMINAL SHAPER, returning CLIENT ARGS -- everything after argv[0]. It does NOT prepend `ssh`,
// because the executable position belongs to the operation declaration: a shaper that returned the
// whole argv would put argv[0] under caller control, which std.operation_argv refuses on the ground
// that a caller deciding WHICH PROGRAM RUNS is strictly worse than a hardcoded list. An earlier
// revision did exactly that and the corpus wall caught it within one CI run.
//
// It takes the sealed context and nothing a caller could widen: there is no
// options parameter to prepend into, so the first-obtained-value hazard has no surface. Every option
// is derived here from the context -- trust from the anchor, credential from the binding, ambient
// suppression unconditionally -- and the principal is projected to an explicit User option rather
// than joined into the destination, so the account being tested is a modeled field rather than text.
fn shape_fleet_ssh_exec(
  context: FleetSshExecutionContext,
  target: SshTarget,
  words: PortableRemoteWords,
) -> List<String> {
  let config_suppression = extdeps.ssh.client_options.openssh_suppress_ambient_configuration_options()
  let derived = concat(
    fleet_openssh_trust_options(policy: context.trust),
    concat(
      fleet_ssh_credential_options(binding: context.credential),
      [
        extdeps.ssh.client_options.User { name: target.principal },
        extdeps.ssh.client_options.BatchMode { enabled: true },
        extdeps.ssh.client_options.ConnectTimeout { seconds: std.measure.second(count: 10) },
      ],
    ),
  )
  concat(
    concat(
      flat_map(config_suppression, o => extdeps.ssh.client_options.serialize_ssh_config_file_flag(option: o)),
      extdeps.ssh.client_options.serialize_ssh_client_options(options: derived),
    ),
    concat([target.endpoint as String, "--"], portable_remote_words_strings(words: words)),
  )
}
